  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 144 PM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nThursday and Friday...As the upper ridge axis remains to the east of \nCO, and another upper low center sits over the CA Baja, the moisture \nplume will remain across NM and CO for Thu, leading to another day \nof widespread precip. Thu is expected to be the coolest day of the \nextended period, with highs forecast to only warm into the 60s for \nthe high valleys, and mid 60s to upper 70s for the plains. The Flash \nFlood Watch for the SW mts will remain in place through Thu evening. \nOn Fri the upper pattern starts to shift slightly, and the upper low \nto the southwest begins to lift out, crossing the state through the \nday, This pattern shift will push the moisture plume to the east, \nbut as the upper disturbance crosses Colorado, it will tap into \navailable moisture and provide for one more day of shower and \nthunderstorm activity for the higher terrain and adjacent plains. \nHigh temps will warm a couple degrees over Thu readings.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...The upper shortwave pushes east, and a ridge \nof high pressure builds in for both Sat and Sun. Long range models \nindicate only isolated afternoon and evening convection, mainly tied \nto the higher terrain, and temps warm back up into the 70s for the \nhigh valleys, and mid 70s to mid 80s for the plains.\n\nMonday...Models are trying to latch onto another upper trough \napproaching for Mon, drawing more moisture into the state and \nproducing another round of precipitation. Stay tuned. Moore",
